00:00:03.409 --> 00:00:07.849 align:start position:0%
this brief scuba diver is recording  sailfish hunting a school of sardines in  order to capture such a scene  researchers spent days on a boat  following frigate birds to pray in  groups as small as four or as large as  70 the fish take turns slashing or  poking the sardine school with their  toothy bills after an attack one fish  will take a break while another takes a  shot  the researchers learned from these  recordings that only 24% of attacks  ended in a sardine snack but 95% ended  up with an injury to a member of the  sardine school injured sardines quickly  tire putting them more at risk of  getting snapped up by one of the selfish  crowd it turns out this is a simplistic  but efficient form of group hunting for  example individuals do not assume  specific roles in the hunt something  seen in more social hunters like  chimpanzees and the fish aren't really  taking turns though the attacks are well  timed they likely are coordinated that  might seem odd in light of these  recordings but when one is facing a  targeted swipe from a sailfish you'd  likely want to get out of the way
 

